# Environment for the krakenkey-connector service (installed by the
# krakenkey-connector package). Upgrades keep your edits to this file.
#
# systemd reads it as an EnvironmentFile: one KEY=value per line, with
# comments on lines of their own. The connector passes these variables on
# to its plugins and commands, so this is where cloud SDK settings go. Only
# root and the krakenkey-connector group can read this file. Each plugin's
# README lists the variables it reads.
#
# On Azure VMs and AWS EC2 instances, prefer a managed identity or an
# instance role: the plugins then get tokens from instance metadata and
# need no secrets here.
#
# Azure (azure-keyvault, azure-appgateway). On a VM with a managed identity:
# AZURE_TOKEN_CREDENTIALS=ManagedIdentityCredential
# For a user-assigned identity, also its client id:
# AZURE_CLIENT_ID=
# Or a service principal with a certificate the service user can read:
# AZURE_TENANT_ID=
# AZURE_CLIENT_ID=
# AZURE_CLIENT_CERTIFICATE_PATH=/etc/krakenkey-connector/azure-sp.pem
#
# AWS (aws-acm):
# AWS_REGION=
# AWS_CONFIG_FILE=/etc/krakenkey-connector/aws-config
# AWS_PROFILE=
#
# Proxy for outbound HTTPS. Keep instance metadata direct.
# HTTPS_PROXY=
# NO_PROXY=169.254.169.254
#
# Do not put an API key or enrollment token here: use a systemd credential
# (LoadCredential=kk_api_key:...) instead. See the README.
